1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,705
Two days after the ice storm,
the Eastside is still dark.

2
00:00:05,705 --> 00:00:12,000
Mrs. Lan is seventy-one. Her heat is off,
and her phone is at twenty-three percent.

3
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:19,611
Help exists: her neighbors raised a fund.
But the form is six pages, in English.

4
00:00:19,611 --> 00:00:24,206
After the Eaton fire, one LA group
took three hundred requests overnight,

5
00:00:24,206 --> 00:00:25,900
in comments on Instagram.

6
00:00:26,100 --> 00:00:31,523
So we built Neighborline:
a hotline, not a dashboard.

7
00:00:31,523 --> 00:00:32,800
She just says it.

8
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:42,400
[Mrs. Lan, in Vietnamese] “Hello, I’m Mrs. Lan, from the
Eastside. After the ice storm,
my house has been without
power for two days now.”

9
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:47,500
She left a voice note,
in Vietnamese. Watch the clock.

10
00:00:48,100 --> 00:00:52,809
She heard it read back in
Vietnamese and said yes.

11
00:00:52,809 --> 00:00:59,003
The group's own rules, not the AI, set
the amount: a hundred and fifty dollars.

12
00:00:59,003 --> 00:01:00,600
Two organizers sign.

13
00:01:02,100 --> 00:01:07,481
Paid, into her Venmo,
with a message in Vietnamese.

14
00:01:07,481 --> 00:01:13,443
Twenty-two point five seconds to the
SUCCESS webhook, on our PayPal stand-in.

15
00:01:13,443 --> 00:01:17,800
The approvals came on cue;
real neighbors take longer.

16
00:01:18,300 --> 00:01:24,300
Rosa asks for groceries, in Spanish.
Same rules, two approvals.

17
00:01:28,702 --> 00:01:32,805
Her email has no PayPal
account yet: unclaimed.

18
00:01:32,805 --> 00:01:35,502
She gets claim help in Spanish.

19
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:41,236
She claims it. Success. Most
payout systems stop at unclaimed;

20
00:01:41,236 --> 00:01:42,800
this one follows through.

21
00:01:43,050 --> 00:01:48,007
Someone posing as the organizers,
with a Venmo handle one letter off?

22
00:01:48,007 --> 00:01:52,550
Approve is disabled on
every phone. No money moves.

23
00:01:52,900 --> 00:01:58,188
Under the hood: the agents that talk
to neighbors have no PayPal tools.

24
00:01:58,188 --> 00:02:02,456
One separate approval service
holds the Payouts credentials,

25
00:02:02,456 --> 00:02:07,700
and it pays only on a record signed with
the passkeys of two different organizers.

26
00:02:08,250 --> 00:02:13,536
The batch ID comes from the signed
approval, so a double tap can't pay twice.

27
00:02:13,536 --> 00:02:18,400
Webhooks are signature-verified, and
every payout is reconciled with PayPal.

28
00:02:18,750 --> 00:02:23,858
For agent builders: the same
guard ships as an MCP server.

29
00:02:23,858 --> 00:02:28,944
An AI agent asking to pay is refused
without two humans' signatures.

30
00:02:28,944 --> 00:02:30,800
With them, it pays.

31
00:02:31,150 --> 00:02:37,524
Donors give with PayPal or Venmo.
The public report never names a neighbor,

32
00:02:37,524 --> 00:02:40,200
and every number comes from the ledger.

33
00:02:40,800 --> 00:02:45,501
That night, one window on
the Eastside is warm again.

34
00:02:45,501 --> 00:02:51,491
Neighborline is open source. Try the
hotline yourself; the link is below.

35
00:02:51,491 --> 00:02:55,700
Because the morning after a storm,
help should sound like your own language.
